ased fivefold in the last 10 years  and the number of positive test results  increased threefold approximately are  you observing similar trend for  pathogens endemic to your region  um that's a fantastic question and  interestingly no what we're seeing with  our Licio rates anyways is they remain  pretty they've remained pretty constant  for the past four to five years we're  not seeing much of a change there I can  say what we have seen more of is when  we've test is we've started testing more  for some of the some of the other  viruses like bourbon virus and Heartland  virus and we're not seeing a ton of it  but we're seeing one or two cases here  and there you know like maybe once a  year so so we're just starting to see  those and those are kind of emerging but  as far as or Licio goes we haven't  really seen an increase and I think it  probably has to do with the fact that  yeah we're very aware of her Licio in  our area we know it's a problem and  we've been testing for it for a long  time and testing pretty often for it so  yeah maybe that explains why our Licio  rates haven't really gone up  thank you it looks like we have time for  one more question  but BC on micro D is the most common  transfusion transmitted pathogen in the  u.s. do you think that implementation of  universal blood screening for abuse yet  is the way to go in order to prevent  transfusion transmitted abuse iosys that  you know that's a really great question  and it's a complicated question because  when we talk about testing the blood  supply we're talking about a very very  different situation and we're actually  using different tests to so we're using  serology and molecular testing now my  general take on this is is kind of  similar to PCR based testing alone one  size doesn't fit all  I think it makes sense to routinely  screen for Babesia in certain areas of  the country where Babesia is very common  in those areas you're going to have a  good positive predictive value and  you're 